<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Guides', href: '/docs/guides' }, { label: 'Managing the Fleet' }]}>
  <h1>Managing the Fleet</h1>

  <p>
    Once your team is running, most of your day-to-day control happens from the control panel.
    This guide covers the core operating moves: seeing status, and steering the fleet.
  </p>

  <h2>Seeing the live status</h2>
  <p>
    The control panel shows you, at a glance:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tickets</strong> — what's open, in progress, awaiting a human, or merged.</li>
    <li><strong>Workers</strong> — which roles are idle, busy, or assigned.</li>
    <li><strong>Pull requests</strong> — what's awaiting merge and what's merged.</li>
    <li><strong>Escalations</strong> — anything that needs a human.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is the same machine-readable state the command-line surface exposes, rendered as a
    dashboard. If you prefer the command surface, the two always agree.
  </p>

  <h2>Steering the fleet</h2>
  <p>
    OpenFlows lets you control <em>how much and what</em> the team works on, live:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pause.</strong> Let anything already in flight finish, but don't pick up new work. Useful when you want to hold the line before a release.</li>
    <li><strong>Drain.</strong> Stop taking on new tickets entirely (while finishing what's active).</li>
    <li><strong>Target.</strong> Focus the team on a specific repository, issue, or label — for example, "only work on the hotfix label right now."</li>
    <li><strong>Resume / normal.</strong> Return to business as usual.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because the controller re-reads its steering state every cycle, these take effect on the
    very next cycle. No restart, no downtime.
  </p>

  <h2>Editing the team (the registry)</h2>
  <p>
    From the panel you can also edit the <strong>agent registry</strong> — how many workers each
    role runs, which model, which skills and tools. Changes are validated (you can't
    accidentally zero out a role) and take effect on the next cycle.
  </p>

  <h2>Recovering when something's stuck</h2>
  <p>
    Most problems are absorbed automatically. When they aren't, you have two levers:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Wait.</strong> A persistent failure is retried every cycle; many "stuck" states clear themselves shortly.</li>
    <li><strong>Reset.</strong> If a team's work is genuinely wedged and automatic recovery has already given up, reset that team back to a clean state. This returns stale or failed tickets to the start and clears the recovery counters, so the team can take a fresh pass.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What you should see</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The new state appears in the panel's status view.</li>
    <li>The team's next cycles honor the change — halting new work if paused, focusing if targeted, rescaling if you changed role counts.</li>
    <li>No restart banner — the team just adjusts smoothly.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Best practices</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Prefer <strong>pause or target</strong> over stopping the whole system when you only want to redirect work.</li>
    <li>Check <strong>escalations</strong> first thing: a parked ticket is your queue of decisions that need a human.</li>
    <li>Use <strong>reset</strong> sparingly and only after automatic recovery has given up — it restarts work, so it isn't free.</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout type="info" title="Stay reachable">
    Alongside steering the fleet, make sure you're reachable when a ticket escalates. See
    <a href="/docs/guides/wiring-notifications">Wiring Notifications</a> and
    <a href="/docs/guides/handling-human-escalations">Handling Human Escalations</a> for the
    other half of operations — acting on what needs you.
  </Callout>
</DocsLayout>
